From wvenable at attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au  Sun May 10 00:50:06 1998
From: wvenable at attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Sun, 10 May 1998 10:20:06 +0930
Subject: Re: [S] bug in predict()
In-Reply-To: <199805091443280810.004C5C6E@funserv.icafe-funclub.de>
References: <199805091443280810.004C5C6E@funserv.icafe-funclub.de>
Message-ID: <9805100050.AA19659@attunga.stats.adelaide.edu.au>

Gerhard Koop writes:
:> There appears to be a serious bug in the predict- function. After
:> having analysed it further I think it lies in an internal routine
:> which is utilized by the model.matrix() function ...  Anyone who
:> is using predict should be warned ...  I'm sorry for this lengthy
:> exposition but I think it shows the matter more clearly.  I sent
:> a copy to support and doc.

Actually no.  One man's serious bug is another woman's handy
feature...  This is, if anything, a failure of the
documentation.  One quirky feature of predict (and there are
many) is the following rule which should be displayed in flashing
neon until it is more widely known:

    Any factors used in a newdata data frame argument of
    precict() *must* have the *same* levels as the original
    factor, and it must have *all* of them.

Occasionally by fluke predict will work if this rule is not
followed, which is one reason why it is a tricky rule.
:> bacteria <- c(1,3,7)
:> # generate factor
:> A <- factor(paste("a", c('1','2','1'), sep=""))
:> B <- factor(paste("b", c('1','1','2'), sep=""))
:> # put everything together
:> raw.df <- data.frame(bacteria, A,B)

:> raw.lm <- lm(bacteria~ A + B,data=raw.df,
:>      model=T, x=T, y=T)
...

Case 1: all levels present, this works fine.

:> # generate factor
:> A <- factor(paste("a", c('1','2'), sep=""))
:> B <- factor(paste("b", c('1','2'), sep=""))
:> # put everything together
:> unbekannt.df  <- data.frame(A,B)
:> predict(raw.lm, unbekannt.df)

...

Case 2: only the first levels present, this works, too, by fluke:

:> # generate factor
:> A <- factor(paste("a", c('1'), sep=""))
:> B <- factor(paste("b", c('1'), sep=""))
:> # put everything together
:> unbekannt.df <- data.frame(A,B)
:> predict(raw.lm,unbekannt.df)

Case 3: only the second level present.  This fails, and pretends
that the second level is the first level:

:> # generate factor
:> A <- factor(paste("a", c('2'), sep=""))
:> B <- factor(paste("b", c('2'), sep=""))
:> # put everything together
:> unbekannt.df <- data.frame(A,B)
:> predict(raw.lm,unbekannt.df)

The correct way to handle it is

> A <- factor(paste("a", c('2'), sep=""), levels=c("a1","a2"))
> B <- factor(paste("b", c('2'), sep=""), levels=c("b1","b2"))
> unbekannt.df <- data.frame(A,B)
> unbekannt.df # ok ?
   A  B 
1 a2 b2
> predict(raw.lm,unbekannt.df)
 1 
 9

and it works fine.

In fact it is not hard to see what is going on.  S-PLUS is
ignoring the printed labels and treating the levels numerically.

Bill Venables.

