        I couldn't let this pass. The easiest way to interface C code (and
Fortran, ASM, Perl, Tcl-Tk, ...) to Splus running under Windows is with
dynamic link libraries (dll's). Writing dll's under 32-bit windows is the
same as writing any other program with a few minor adjustments. The days
of the evil 16-bit dll are gone. The dll has the added advantage that you
can write your functions, write a separate Splus wrapper, a separate
Visual Basic wrapper, ..., and use the same dll. Next time you want that
function in a C program (or a Fortran program, or a ...) it is there
compiled and debugged for you to use. Watcom make fine compilers I am
sure, but even if I had one I would use it to write dll's.

