Subject: [S] GAM models with lo() terms; interpretation.
Message-ID: <199805180155.LAA06204@leonard.anu.edu.au>

I'm running S-PLUS 3.4 under Unix, under SunOS 5.3.

I'm using a gam model to regress logweight on lo(logheight), lo(age),
and altitude, in a modest sized data set.  My concern is that
summary.gam() and summary.lm() give what seem inconsistent views of
the output, especialy allowing for the fact that summary.lm is likely
to exaggerate statistical significance.

I've been making extensive use of these models; so feel a concern to
understand their properties and traps.  Here's the output from
summary.gam()

>>>>>>>
> summary(u1.0)

Call: gam(formula = logweight ~ lo(logheight) + lo(age) + altitude, data = 
hilomet, 
        subset = here & (hilomet$sex == "male"), na.action = na.omit)
Deviance Residuals:
        Min          1Q       Median         3Q       Max 
 -0.1982253 -0.04852361 -0.003986395 0.05367157 0.2376238

(Dispersion Parameter for Gaussian family taken to be 0.0054559 )

    Null Deviance: 89.90029 on 308 degrees of freedom

Residual Deviance: 1.633921 on 299.4788 degrees of freedom

Number of Local Scoring Iterations: 1 

DF for Terms and F-values for Nonparametric Effects

              Df Npar Df   Npar F        Pr(F) 
  (Intercept)  1                              
lo(logheight)  1     3.0 30.39156 0.000000e+00
      lo(age)  1     2.5 32.58834 5.551115e-16
     altitude  1                              
---------

So it appears that there is a very strong nonlinear effect of age.
However summary.lm(), which one might expect to exaggerate the
significance, seems relatively unimpressed by the contribution 
from the transformed value of age.  Here's what it gives:

>>>>>>
> summary.lm(u1.0)

Call: gam(formula = logweight ~ lo(logheight) + lo(age) + altitude, 
data = hilomet, subset = here & (hilomet$sex == "male"), na.action = na.omit)
Residuals:
     Min       1Q    Median      3Q    Max 
 -0.1982 -0.04852 -0.003986 0.05367 0.2376

Coefficients:
                  Value Std. Error   t value  Pr(>|t|) 
  (Intercept)    3.5200    0.0080   441.0709    0.0000
lo(logheight)   11.3218    0.1460    77.5355    0.0000
      lo(age)    0.3785    0.1683     2.2490    0.0252
     altitude   -0.0001    0.0000    -3.5145    0.0005

Residual standard error: 0.07386 on 299.478758987789 degrees of freedom
Multiple R-Squared: 0.9782 
F-statistic: 5383 on 3 and 299.478758987789 degrees of freedom, 
the p-value is 0 

Correlation of Coefficients:
              (Intercept) lo(logheight) lo(age) 
lo(logheight)  0.0583                          
      lo(age) -0.1190     -0.7695              
     altitude -0.8457     -0.0239        0.0469

---------
The plot of lo(age) versus age looks pretty much like a backward
slanting mirror image of an "N", i. e. close to a cubic.
If one fits poly(age,3) in place of lo(age), and makes the
comparison with poly(age,1), the F-test for the 
(quadratic +cubic) components has p=0.00000014.

The quadratic component has p=.0026 and the cubic component p=0.2 .
Rugplots show that the data are fairly dense in the interesting
places.  Residual plots seem fairly ok. I omitted three
negative outliers.

----------

The gam model has surely over-fitted.  There is a relatively low
correlation (-.77) between lo(age) and lo(logheight), much lower than
with the polynimial terms in the polynomial model (-.97, .94, -.78).
So why is the over-fitting sufficiently serious to mask or almost mask
the significance of the non-linear component of lo(age)?

I guess the lesson is that one needs to use every available clue
to what may be happening.  I wonder what insights contributors
may have.

Incidentally, StatSci really does need to do something about the
299.478758987789 degrees of freedom.

