From: ckeller at aicos.com (Christian Keller)
Date: Tue, 19 May 1998 19:19:21 +0200
Subject: Re: [S] plot 2+3
References: <Pine.ULT.3.91.980519100823.4351H-100000@mathfs.math.montana.edu>
Message-ID: <3561BF19.7BFB@aicos.com>

A much simpler solution (in my opinion) is to use split.screen().

Example:

x <- matrix(rnorm(120),20,6)

split.screen(figs=matrix(c(0,0.5,0.5,1, 0.5,1,0.5,1,
                           0,1/3,0,0.5, 1/3,2/3,0,0.5, 2/3,1,0,0.5),
               ncol=4, byrow=T))
screen(1)
plot(x[,1:2])
screen(2)
plot(x[,2:3])
screen(3)
plot(x[,3:4])
screen(4)
plot(x[,4:5])
screen(5)
plot(x[,5:6])
close.screen(all=T)

See help(split.screen) for an explanation of the argument figs.

