From JPNOLAN at american.edu  Tue May 19 22:08:49 1998
From: JPNOLAN at american.edu (John Nolan)
Date: Tue, 19 May 1998 18:08:49 -0400
Subject: Re: [S] stable random variable generation
In-Reply-To: <199805191736.TAA27450@ieva.maf.vu.lt>
Message-ID: <199805192239.RAA21720@wubios.wustl.edu>

The Chambers, Mallows, Stuck algorithm is the best general method
for generating stable r.v.  It is also quite fast; the complaint
about it being slow inside S-Plus is probably due to other things
the user was doing with the simulations.

For the record:
1. RSTAB blows up occasionally with a divide by zero.
2. There were some minor coefficients wrong inside the original
version of RSTAB.  The fortran code I got from Chambers several
years ago still had those incorrect DATA statements.
3. RSTAB returns a normalized stable r.v. in Zolotarev's (M)
parameterization, NOT the parameterization most people use.

The program STABLE contains a version of RSTAB without these
features.  It also performs accurate stable density, cdf and
quantile calculations for a general stable distribution.
It includes a fast spline interpolation for stable densities
(with alpha > .5) and maximum likelihood estimation of stable
parameters.  So heavy tailed, skewed data sets can be fit
with stable models in a matter of seconds.

A Win95 version of this can be downloaded from
my webpage (URL at bottom).   Click on the link to stable
distributions and see what's there.
Also on the webpage are some papers about stable distributions:
one on max. likelihood est., one on parameterizations, etc.

