Suppose the data were in a data.frame called orth1 in the form you
describe.  You could convert to the "unstacked" form with
S> orth2 <- data.frame(distance = unlist(orth1[, -(1:2)]))
S> orth2$subject <- rep(orth1$subject, 4)
S> orth2$sex <- as.factor(rep(orth1$sex, 4))
S> orth2$age <- rep(c(8,10,12,14), rep(nrow(orth1), 4))

That code is customized for that particular data set.  It also
produces an data frame where the data from each age are in adjacent
rows rather than the data from each subject in adjacent rows.  If you
prefer the data for each subject to be adjacent you can reorder the
rows by 
S> orth2 <- orth2[ order(orth2$subject), ]
The order of the rows doesn't matter in lme but it may be easier to
read this way.

There is (well, actually, there will be) a function balancedGrouped in
version 3.0 of the nlme library that takes just the data matrix for a
balanced design like this and produces a groupedData object of the
form to be used by lme.

> (2) The output of linear mixed effects models gives parameter estimates,
> standard error, and z ratio. I can compute p-value using the z ratio. Is
> there any way to produce p-value within S-plus by saving the column of
> z-ratio?

I think it is the output of summary(lmeFit) that produces the
z-ratios.  The z-ratios are the third column of
 summary(lmeFit)$fixed.table

An alternative is to trap the value of anova(lmeFit) and look at those
columns.

In version 3.0 of the nlme library the p-values are calculated and
printed as part of summary(lmeFit).  The beta test version of this
library is available at
              http://franz.stat.wisc.edu/pub/NLME/Beta/
